After sorting both arrays from largest to smallest, the biggest pair starts at `(0, 0)`. From a pair `(i, j)`, the next useful positions are `(i + 1, j)` and `(i, j + 1)`. A max heap always gives the largest remaining sum.

The `visited` table is important. Both moves can reach the same position, and marking positions prevents adding a pair more than once.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <queue>
#include <algorithm>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
  vector<int> maxSumCombinations(vector<int> &nums1, vector<int> &nums2, int k) {
    sort(nums1.rbegin(), nums1.rend());
    sort(nums2.rbegin(), nums2.rend());

    using State = pair<int, pair<int, int>>;
    priority_queue<State> maxHeap;
    vector<vector<bool>> visited(nums1.size(), vector<bool>(nums2.size(), false));
    vector<int> answer;

    if (nums1.empty() || nums2.empty() || k == 0) {
      return answer;
    }

    maxHeap.push({nums1[0] + nums2[0], {0, 0}});
    visited[0][0] = true;

    while (!maxHeap.empty() && static_cast<int>(answer.size()) < k) {
      State current = maxHeap.top();
      maxHeap.pop();

      int sum = current.first;
      int row = current.second.first;
      int column = current.second.second;
      answer.push_back(sum);

      if (row + 1 < static_cast<int>(nums1.size()) && !visited[row + 1][column]) {
        maxHeap.push({nums1[row + 1] + nums2[column], {row + 1, column}});
        visited[row + 1][column] = true;
      }

      if (column + 1 < static_cast<int>(nums2.size()) && !visited[row][column + 1]) {
        maxHeap.push({nums1[row] + nums2[column + 1], {row, column + 1}});
        visited[row][column + 1] = true;
      }
    }

    return answer;
  }
};
```

Dry run with sorted arrays `[7, 4]` and `[6, 2]`: the heap starts with `13`. It then considers `11` from `(1, 0)` and `9` from `(0, 1)`. With `k = 3`, the output is `[13, 11, 9]`.

Time complexity: `O(n log n + m log m + k log(n * m))`.

Space complexity: `O(n * m)` for the visited table and heap.

A direct version is useful here because every pair is allowed: take one value from `nums1` and one from `nums2`, store their sum, then sort all sums from largest to smallest. The first `k` values are the answer.

This keeps the code easy to check, but it stores all `n * m` combinations, so it is not the best choice for large arrays.

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public int[] maxSumCombinations(int[] nums1, int[] nums2, int k) {
        int totalPairs = nums1.length * nums2.length;
        int[] sums = new int[totalPairs];
        int index = 0;

        for (int first : nums1) {
            for (int second : nums2) {
                sums[index++] = first + second;
            }
        }

        Arrays.sort(sums);
        int[] answer = new int[k];

        for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {
            answer[i] = sums[sums.length - 1 - i];
        }

        return answer;
    }
}
```

For a small dry run, `nums1 = [4, 2]` and `nums2 = [3, 1]` create sums `[7, 5, 5, 3]`. With `k = 3`, the result is `[7, 5, 5]`.

Time complexity: `O(n * m + (n * m) log(n * m))`.

Space complexity: `O(n * m)` for all pair sums.

Each sorted row has decreasing sums. For a fixed value from `nums1`, the largest sum uses `nums2[0]`, then the next one uses `nums2[1]`, and so on. I put the first item from every row into a max heap and advance only the row that was just removed.

This avoids building the full list of pair sums. It also handles repeated values normally because heap entries represent pair positions, not only the sum value.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution {

    private class Entry {
        public int Sum;
        public int Row;
        public int Column;

        public Entry(int sum, int row, int column) {
            Sum = sum;
            Row = row;
            Column = column;
        }
    }

    public List<int> MaxSumCombinations(List<int> nums1, List<int> nums2, int k) {
        nums1.Sort((a, b) => b.CompareTo(a));
        nums2.Sort((a, b) => b.CompareTo(a));

        var answer = new List<int>();
        if (nums1.Count == 0 || nums2.Count == 0 || k == 0) {
            return answer;
        }

        var heap = new PriorityQueue<Entry, int>();

        for (int row = 0; row < nums1.Count; row++) {
            heap.Enqueue(
                new Entry(nums1[row] + nums2[0], row, 0),
                -(nums1[row] + nums2[0])
            );
        }

        while (heap.Count > 0 && answer.Count < k) {
            Entry current = heap.Dequeue();
            answer.Add(current.Sum);

            int nextColumn = current.Column + 1;
            if (nextColumn < nums2.Count) {
                int nextSum = nums1[current.Row] + nums2[nextColumn];
                heap.Enqueue(
                    new Entry(nextSum, current.Row, nextColumn),
                    -nextSum
                );
            }
        }

        return answer;
    }
}
```

An edge case is a one-element array. If `nums1 = [10]`, the heap just walks across `nums2`, producing its sums in descending order. The input lists are sorted in place, so this implementation does modify them.

Time complexity: `O(n log n + m log m + (n + k) log n)`.

Space complexity: `O(n)` for the heap, apart from the returned list.

This version repeatedly finds the largest unused pair by scanning the whole pair table. After selecting one pair, that exact position is marked so duplicate values at different positions are still counted correctly.

It is slower than a heap, but the greedy step is very easy to follow: every round chooses the largest sum that has not been selected yet.

```java
class Solution {
    public int[] maxSumCombinations(int[] nums1, int[] nums2, int k) {
        int n = nums1.length;
        int m = nums2.length;
        boolean[][] used = new boolean[n][m];
        int[] answer = new int[k];

        for (int picked = 0; picked < k; picked++) {
            int bestRow = -1;
            int bestColumn = -1;
            int bestSum = Integer.MIN_VALUE;

            for (int row = 0; row < n; row++) {
                for (int column = 0; column < m; column++) {
                    if (!used[row][column]) {
                        int sum = nums1[row] + nums2[column];
                        if (bestRow == -1 || sum > bestSum) {
                            bestSum = sum;
                            bestRow = row;
                            bestColumn = column;
                        }
                    }
                }
            }

            used[bestRow][bestColumn] = true;
            answer[picked] = bestSum;
        }

        return answer;
    }
}
```

For `nums1 = [5, 1]`, `nums2 = [4, 2]`, the first scan selects `9` from `(5, 4)`. The next scan selects `7`, and then another `7` from the other pair position. This shows why positions are marked instead of only storing sums.

Time complexity: `O(k * n * m)`.

Space complexity: `O(n * m)` for the used table.

Using Set`<`String`>` is suboptimal and little inefficient.
For better optimization we can use **Set`<`Long`>` visited** and we can use **((long) i << 32) | j** to create keys.
## Solution
```java[]
class Solution {
    public int[] maxSumCombinations(int[] nums1, int[] nums2, int k) {
        if (nums1.length == 0 || nums2.length == 0 || k == 0) {
            return new int[0];
        }

        Arrays.sort(nums1);
        Arrays.sort(nums2);

        int n = nums1.length;
        int m = nums2.length;

        PriorityQueue<int[]> pq = new PriorityQueue<>(
            (a, b) -> b[0] - a[0]
        );

        Set<Long> visited = new HashSet<>();

        pq.offer(new int[]{nums1[n - 1] + nums2[m - 1], n - 1, m - 1});
        visited.add(encode(n - 1, m - 1));

        int[] res = new int[k];
        int idx = 0;

        while (idx < k && !pq.isEmpty()) {
            int[] cur = pq.poll();
            res[idx++] = cur[0];

            int i = cur[1], j = cur[2];

            if (i - 1 >= 0 && visited.add(encode(i - 1, j))) {
                pq.offer(new int[]{nums1[i - 1] + nums2[j], i - 1, j});
            }

            if (j - 1 >= 0 && visited.add(encode(i, j - 1))) {
                pq.offer(new int[]{nums1[i] + nums2[j - 1], i, j - 1});
            }
        }

        return res;
    }

    private long encode(int i, int j) {
        return ((long) i << 32) | j;
    }
}

